#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:4
msgid "Install from source (GNU Linux and macOS)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:7
msgid "Contents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:9
msgid ""
"This page describes the build and installation of PyBaMM from the source "
"code, available on GitHub. Note that this is **not the recommended approach "
"for most users** and should be reserved to people wanting to participate in "
"the development of PyBaMM, or people who really need to use bleeding-edge "
"feature(s) not yet available in the latest released version. If you do not "
"fall in the two previous categories, you would be better off installing "
"PyBaMM using pip or conda."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:11
msgid ""
"Lastly, familiarity with the Python ecosystem is recommended (pip or uv, "
"virtualenvs). Here is a gentle introduction/refresher: `Python Virtual "
"Environments: A Primer <https://realpython.com/python-virtual-environments-a-"
"primer/>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:16
msgid "Prerequisites"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:18
msgid ""
"The following instructions are valid for both GNU/Linux distributions and "
"MacOS. If you are running Windows, consider using the `Windows Subsystem for "
"Linux (WSL) <https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/wsl/install-win10>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:21
msgid "To obtain the PyBaMM source code, clone the GitHub repository"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:23
msgid ""
"git clone --recurse-submodules https://github.com/pybamm-team/PyBaMM.git"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:27
msgid ""
"or download the source archive on the repository's homepage. The ``--recurse-"
"submodules`` flag fetches the SUNDIALS and SuiteSparse sources used to build "
"the in-repo IDAKLU solver (``pybammsolvers``); if you already cloned without "
"it, run ``git submodule update --init``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:32
msgid "To install PyBaMM, you will need:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:34
msgid "Python 3 (PyBaMM supports versions 3.10 — 3.14)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:35
msgid "The Python headers file for your current Python version."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:36
msgid "A BLAS library (for instance `openblas <https://www.openblas.net/>`_)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:37
msgid "A C compiler (ex: ``gcc``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:38
msgid "A Fortran compiler (ex: ``gfortran``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:39
msgid ""
"``graphviz`` (optional), if you wish to build the documentation locally."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:40
msgid ""
"``pandoc`` (optional) to convert the example Jupyter notebooks when building "
"the documentation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:42
msgid "You can install the above with"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:46
msgid ""
"sudo apt install python3.X python3.X-dev libopenblas-dev gcc gfortran "
"graphviz cmake make pandoc"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:50
msgid "Where ``X`` is the version sub-number."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:54
msgid "brew install python openblas gcc gfortran graphviz libomp cmake pandoc"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:60
msgid ""
"If you are using some other linux distribution you can install the "
"equivalent packages for ``python3, cmake, gcc, gfortran, openblas, pandoc``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:62
msgid ""
"On Windows, you can install ``graphviz`` using the `Chocolatey <https://"
"chocolatey.org/>`_ package manager, or follow the instructions on the "
"`graphviz website <https://graphviz.org/download/>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:64
msgid ""
"Finally, we recommend using `Nox <https://nox.thea.codes/en/stable/>`_ for "
"running tests. You can install it with"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:69
msgid "uv tool install nox"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:75
msgid "python3.X -m pip install --user nox"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:79
msgid ""
"Note that running ``nox`` will create new virtual environments for you to "
"use, so you do not need to create one yourself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:81
msgid ""
"Depending on your operating system, you may or may not have ``pip`` "
"installed along Python. If ``pip`` is not found, you probably want to "
"install the ``python3-pip`` package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:88
msgid "Installing PyBaMM"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:90
msgid ""
"You should now have everything ready to build and install PyBaMM "
"successfully."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:94
msgid ""
"``uv sync`` and ``nox`` build the in-repo IDAKLU solver (``pybammsolvers``) "
"from source. The first such build compiles the vendored SUNDIALS and "
"SuiteSparse into ``packages/pybammsolvers/.idaklu`` automatically — this "
"takes a few minutes the first time, then is cached. You only need the "
"submodules present (fetched by ``--recurse-submodules`` above, or ``git "
"submodule update --init``) and ``make``, ``cmake``, and a C/Fortran compiler "
"installed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:102
msgid ""
"To use a system SUNDIALS/SuiteSparse instead of the vendored build, set "
"``SUNDIALS_ROOT`` and ``SuiteSparse_ROOT``. The manual ``pip`` install below "
"and the released ``pip install pybamm`` both pull a prebuilt "
"``pybammsolvers`` wheel from PyPI, so they skip this build entirely."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:108
msgid "Using ``uv`` (recommended)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:110
msgid ""
"`uv <https://docs.astral.sh/uv/>`_ is a fast Python package manager that "
"supports lockfile-based installs. PyBaMM includes a ``uv.lock`` file to "
"ensure reproducible environments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:113
msgid ""
"# in the PyBaMM/ directory\n"
"uv sync --extra all --group dev --group docs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:118
msgid ""
"This creates a virtual environment ``.venv/`` inside the ``PyBaMM/`` "
"directory with PyBaMM installed in editable mode, all optional dependencies, "
"and development tools."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:121
#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:151
msgid "You can now activate the environment with"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:125
msgid "source .venv/bin/activate"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:131
msgid ".venv\\Scripts\\activate.bat"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:135
#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:165
msgid "and run the tests to check your installation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:138
msgid "Using ``Nox``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:140
msgid ""
"# in the PyBaMM/ directory\n"
"nox -s dev"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:146
msgid ""
"It is recommended to use ``--verbose`` or ``-v`` to see outputs of all "
"commands run."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:148
msgid ""
"This creates a virtual environment ``venv/`` inside the ``PyBaMM/`` "
"directory. It comes ready with PyBaMM and some useful development tools like "
"`pre-commit <https://pre-commit.com/>`_ and `ruff <https://beta.ruff.rs/docs/"
">`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:155
msgid "source venv/bin/activate"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:161
msgid "venv\\Scripts\\activate.bat"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:168
msgid "Manual install (pip)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:170
msgid ""
"The ``pybamm`` package lives at ``packages/pybamm`` within the repository. "
"From the ``PyBaMM/`` directory, you can install it using"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:173
msgid "pip install ./packages/pybamm"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:177
msgid ""
"If you intend to contribute to the development of PyBaMM, it is convenient "
"to install in \"editable mode\", along with all the optional dependencies "
"and useful tools for development and documentation. The ``dev`` and ``docs`` "
"dependency groups live in the package's ``pyproject.toml``, so reference "
"them by path:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:182
msgid ""
"pip install -e ./packages/pybamm[all] --group packages/pybamm/pyproject.toml:"
"dev --group packages/pybamm/pyproject.toml:docs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:186
msgid ""
"If you are using ``zsh`` or ``tcsh``, you would need to quote the extras to "
"avoid pattern matching:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:188
msgid ""
"pip install -e './packages/pybamm[all]' --group packages/pybamm/pyproject."
"toml:dev --group packages/pybamm/pyproject.toml:docs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:192
msgid ""
"Before you start contributing to PyBaMM, please read the `contributing "
"guidelines <https://github.com/pybamm-team/PyBaMM/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING."
"md>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:196
msgid "Running the tests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:199
msgid "Using Nox (recommended)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:201
msgid ""
"You can use ``Nox`` to run the unit tests and example notebooks in isolated "
"virtual environments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:203
msgid "The default command"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:205
msgid "nox"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:209
msgid ""
"will run pre-commit, install ``Linux`` and ``macOS`` dependencies, and run "
"the unit tests. This can take several minutes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:212
msgid "To just run the unit tests, use"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:214
msgid "nox -s unit"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:218
msgid "Similarly, to run the integration tests, use"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:220
msgid "nox -s integration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:224
msgid "Finally, to run the unit and the integration suites sequentially, use"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:226
msgid "nox -s tests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:231
msgid "Using pytest"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:233
msgid "You can run unit tests for PyBaMM using"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:235
msgid ""
"# in the PyBaMM/ directory\n"
"pytest -m unit"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:241
msgid ""
"The above uses pytest (in your current Python environment) to run the unit "
"tests. This can take a few minutes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:244
msgid "You can also use pytest to run the doctests:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:246
msgid "pytest --doctest-plus src"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:250
msgid ""
"Refer to the `testing <https://docs.pybamm.org/en/stable/source/user_guide/"
"contributing.html#testing>`_ docs to find out more ways to test PyBaMM using "
"pytest."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:253
msgid "How to build the PyBaMM documentation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:255
msgid "The documentation is built using"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:257
msgid "nox -s docs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:261
msgid ""
"This will build the documentation and serve it locally (thanks to `sphinx-"
"autobuild <https://github.com/GaretJax/sphinx-autobuild>`_) for preview. The "
"preview will be updated automatically following changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:265
msgid "Doctests, examples, and coverage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:267
msgid ""
"``Nox`` can also be used to run doctests, run examples, and generate a "
"coverage report using:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:269
msgid ""
"``nox -s examples``: Run the Jupyter notebooks in ``docs/source/examples/"
"notebooks/``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:270
msgid ""
"``nox -s examples -- <path-to-notebook-1.ipynb> <path-to_notebook-2."
"ipynb>``: Run specific Jupyter notebooks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:271
msgid "``nox -s scripts``: Run the example scripts in ``examples/scripts/``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:272
msgid "``nox -s doctests``: Run doctests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:273
msgid ""
"``nox -s coverage``: Measure current test coverage and generate a coverage "
"report."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:274
msgid ""
"``nox -s quick``: Run integration tests, unit tests, and doctests "
"sequentially."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:277
msgid "Extra tips while using ``Nox``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:279
msgid "Here are some additional useful commands you can run with ``Nox``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:281
msgid ""
"``--verbose or -v``: Enables verbose mode, providing more detailed output "
"during the execution of Nox sessions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:282
msgid ""
"``--list or -l``: Lists all available Nox sessions and their descriptions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:283
msgid ""
"``--stop-on-first-error``: Stops the execution of Nox sessions immediately "
"after the first error or failure occurs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:284
msgid ""
"``--envdir <path>``: Specifies the directory where Nox creates and manages "
"the virtual environments used by the sessions. In this case, the directory "
"is set to ``<path>``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:285
msgid ""
"``--install-only``: Skips the test execution and only performs the "
"installation step defined in the Nox sessions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:286
msgid ""
"``--nocolor``: Disables the color output in the console during the execution "
"of Nox sessions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:287
msgid ""
"``--report output.json``: Generates a JSON report of the Nox session "
"execution and saves it to the specified file, in this case, \"output.json\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:288
msgid ""
"``nox -s docs --non-interactive``: Builds the documentation without serving "
"it locally (using ``sphinx-build`` instead of ``sphinx-autobuild``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:291
msgid "Troubleshooting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:293
msgid ""
"**Problem:** I have made edits to source files in PyBaMM, but these are not "
"being used when I run my Python script."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/installation/install-from-source.rst:296
msgid ""
"**Solution:** Make sure you have installed PyBaMM in editable mode. If using "
"``uv``, run ``uv sync`` (editable by default). If using ``pip``, use the ``-"
"e`` flag: ``pip install -e ./packages/pybamm``. This sets the installed "
"location of the source files to your current directory."
msgstr ""
